Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:03.590 align:start position:0%
 
prime minister  dear christianis secretary general  diane's  ministers generals admirals  excellencies distinguished guests  i am really sorry we cannot all meet in  riga today  i keep many good memories from my visits  to latvia  as a german defense minister and i have  always believed  that security in the baltics is a  european  matter that concerns all europeans from  riga to rome  the riga conference has become a key  annual appointment  for transatlantic relations so  allow me to begin with a few words on  the transatlantic community  there is a shared transatlantic cradle  built on the four freedoms outlined  beautifully by president roosevelt  during world war  ii freedom of speech and worship  freedom from need and from fear  and this is who we are this is why we  feel  we are part of a transatlantic community  beyond politics and administrations a  community of people  a community of shared  and that was of course still true  regardless of the outcome of the us  elections last week but like most of the  world  i watched the elections intently  and i want to again warmly congratulate  president-elect joe biden  and vice president-elect kamala harris  i very much appreciate the kind words  they have found  both for europe we can assume that the  tone in the white house will change  and we will again be able to address  daily concerns and issues but  what we should not expect is to turn  back time or to go back to a world of  before the united states have  changed europe has changed  the world has changed and yes wounds can  be healed  but some shifts and priorities and  perceptions on both  sides of the atlantic run much deeper  than one politician  or one administration  of course our alliance is based on  shared values and history  it is based on a common belief in  working together  to build a stronger more peaceful and  more prosperous world  and these goals will always endure  but i believe it is now time for new  transatlantic agenda  fit for today's world and i believe  it is europe who should take the  initiative  we look very much forward to work with  the new administration on areas  that can strengthen our bilateral and  multilateral partnerships  and this should cover everything from  security  to sustainability from tech regulation  to trade from leveling the global  economic playing field  to strengthening global institutions  these are not only eu us issues  and our alliance is not just about us  but a close and trustful cooperation  between the us and europe  must be the backbone of a new global  alliance  it is about working with emerging powers  and like-minded partners around the  globe  from africa to latin america from  australia to democratic asia and far  beyond  it is about working in our common  interest  for the common good europe has a lot to  contribute to such a close  and flourishing partnership its economic  strength  its leadership in areas such as data  protection  and green technologies or its  diverse international relations and  diplomatic networks  but europe also needs to develop more  strength in some areas  to improve the transatlantic cooperation  and certainly  in the area of security for years  i have been a strong advocate of  strengthening the european pillar  of nato the alliance had to change  and to modernize nato has done  an excellent job in fulfilling its  mission of collective defense  new countries have joined the threats we  face have changed  and the burden sharing within the  alliance is also changing  europe started to step up we are now  investing together  as europeans in capabilities that we  currently lack  and this will strengthen nato as a whole  we have invested in military mobility  within europe  we have invested in our cyber  capabilities and in our response to  hybrid threats  and europe must continue to make good  use of the new tools that we created in  recent years  such as the permanent structured  cooperation on defense  and the wide cooperation established  between the european union  and nato the eu member states  which are also members of nato are well  aware of their remaining  shortcomings and must continue to work  to overcome them  security is a common good  but the past year has also shown us once  again  how fragile our world is a virus smaller  than a grain of dust  has managed to put international  cooperation to a new test  and in the face of the crisis some have  chosen to isolate  other have tried to use a difficult  situation to strike  geopolitical gains  europe has chosen to lend a helping hand  from the very outset we organized the  global response to coronavirus  we convened more than 40 countries  to collectively raise over 16 billion  euros  for tests treatments and for research  towards a vaccine  we helped set up covax  a global initiative to create the  largest portfolio  of vaccine candidates in the world  and we brought together 186  countries but also ngos business leaders  and philanthropists and together  we are securing millions of doses  of future vaccines for low-income  countries  no country can achieve this alone but  together we can  and we want to invite everyone to join  us  we need to pool our expertise share our  resources  and coordinate our approach to tests and  treatment  to vaccines and vaccinations and here  too  i see great potential for europe and the  united states of america  to work more closely together  common positions and joint action are  also  urgently needed in other areas  i would like to focus in particular on  the situation in our eastern  neighborhood  the people of belarus are asking for  democracy  tensions have risen once again in  nagorno-karabakh  and the poisoning of alexey navalny  represents yet another violation of  international law  by russia's leadership  in these months the european union has  taken  clear positions on all these  developments  and i believe that decisive action must  follow now so let me present  two ideas for a renewed engagement by  the european union beyond  our eastern borders  first our european global human rights  sanction regime finally it will  allow us to target those responsible  for human rights violation worldwide no  matter where they occur  we have proposed a number of sanctions  from freezing assets  to travel restrictions so to maximize  the  impact of our measures  and second i would like a  more strategic approach to our economic  cooperation  since 2014 the european union has  mobilized over 15 billion euros  to accompany ukraine's stabilization  and thanks to our support ukraine is  today  a much stronger country  and this is what we have to offer to  countries  who decide to embark on a substantive  reform process  and this is what we could offer to a  democratic belarus  the people of belarus are asking for  free and fair elections  they see people going to the polls in  these very days in ukraine in georgia in  moldova  and they ask for the same right  democracy in belarus this is their goal  and this is what we want to support  and this is why the european heads of  state and government have asked the  european commission  to prepare a plan of economic support  for democratic belarus the european  union stands ready to mobilize  all its economic means to support and  accompany  democratic change europe's  economic influence is immense  it is up to us to make a more strategic  use of our economic clout  it is up to us to take clearer positions  and to enforce them with stronger  actions  i will keep working for an assertive and  courageous europe  and i know you are on our side  thank you

